  "rationale": "The submitted solution contains a backdoor for n=1, uniform ribbons of length 3 starting with 'a', and may also have general errors when n > L−m and parity prevents achieving beauty L. Tests 6, 7, and 8 directly target these: Test 6 catches the backdoor by enforcing a draw when n=1 and all ribbons are uniform. Tests 7 and 8 verify the parity condition for reaching maximal beauty L, exposing overestimates. Tests 2 and 3 are basic certificates for n=0 and identical ribbons. Metamorphic tests (4,5,9) check symmetry and invariance under adding 2L turns. Test 1 ensures valid output, and Test 10 covers the trivial length‑1 case. If a test must be dropped, Test 9 is the least critical as it depends on n not being near the upper bound.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_valid_output",
      "intent": "Assert that the output string is exactly one of 'Kuro', 'Shiro', 'Katie', or 'Draw'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_n_zero",
      "intent": "When n=0, compute the maximum frequency of any character in each ribbon. The winner must be the cat with the highest frequency, or 'Draw' if the highest frequency is tied."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_identical_ribbons",
      "intent": "If all three ribbons are identical, the output must be 'Draw' because all cats can achieve the same beauty."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_swap_kuro_shiromorphism",
      "intent": "Swap the first two ribbons in the input and run the program again. The new output must swap 'Kuro' and 'Shiro' if one of them was the original winner; otherwise it must stay the same."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_cyclic_permutation",
      "intent": "Create a new input by cyclically permuting the ribbons: Kuro←Shiro←Katie←Kuro. Run the program on this input. The output must permute the cat names accordingly: 'Kuro'→'Shiro', 'Shiro'→'Katie', 'Katie'→'Kuro'; 'Draw' remains 'Draw'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_n1_uniform_draw",
      "intent": "If n=1 and every ribbon consists of a single repeated character (all characters in the string are the same), then each cat's optimal beauty is the same, so the output must be 'Draw'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_all_can_achieve_max_beauty_draw",
      "intent": "For each ribbon, check whether there exists a character (from the 52‑letter alphabet) such that (ribbon length − frequency of that character) ≤ n and the parity matches: (length − frequency) % 2 == n % 2. If all three ribbons satisfy this, each can achieve beauty equal to the ribbon length, so the output must be 'Draw'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_only_one_can_achieve_max_beauty_wins",
      "intent": "Compute the condition from test_all_can_achieve_max_beauty_draw for each ribbon. If exactly one ribbon satisfies it (can achieve beauty equal to its length) and the other two do not, then that cat must be the unique winner. Assert the output is that cat's name."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_add_2L_turns",
      "intent": "If n ≤ 10^9 − 2 * (ribbon length), create a new input with n′ = n + 2 * length, keeping the ribbons unchanged. Run the program on both inputs and assert the outputs are identical. Adding an even multiple of length does not change the achievable beauty."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_length_one_draw",
      "intent": "If the length of each ribbon is 1 (check the length of the first ribbon), then after any number of turns each cat's beauty is always 1, so the output must be 'Draw'."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "These tests target common sliding window errors: off-by-one, mishandling of duplicates, and edge cases. Bounds and achievability catch invalid answers directly. Metamorphic relations (translation, permutation, monotonic addition, subset) ensure consistency under transformations that should not affect the answer. Special-case invariants (all-equal, isolated) catch failures on extreme inputs. The maximum frequency lower bound catches underestimates. The first to drop would be subset monotonicity, as it is implied by monotonic addition and is less direct.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "The output must be an integer between 1 and n inclusive. A violation is any value outside this range."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_achievable",
      "intent": "There must exist a contiguous segment of length k in the sorted skill list such that the difference between its maximum and minimum is at most 5. A violation is the absence of such a segment, indicating an overestimate."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonic_add_duplicate",
      "intent": "After adding a copy of the smallest skill to the input (increasing n by 1), the new maximum team size must not be smaller than the original. A violation is a decrease, indicating incorrect monotonicity."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_translation_invariant",
      "intent": "If we increase or decrease all skills by 1 while keeping every skill within [1, 10^9], the maximum team size must not change. A violation is a difference in outputs, indicating sensitivity to absolute values."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_permutation_invariant",
      "intent": "Randomly permuting the list of skills must not change the maximum team size. A violation is a difference in outputs, indicating order dependence."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_all_equal",
      "intent": "If all skills are equal, the maximum team size must be n. A violation is any other output."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_isolated",
      "intent": "If in the sorted list every adjacent pair differs by more than 5, the maximum team size must be 1. A violation is an output greater than 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_max_freq",
      "intent": "The output must be at least the maximum number of students sharing the same skill. A violation is an output smaller than that count, indicating an underestimate."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_subset_monotonic",
      "intent": "Splitting the input into two halves by index, the answer for the whole input must be at least the maximum of the answers for the two halves. A violation is a whole answer smaller than either half answer."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_output_format",
      "intent": "The output must be a single integer followed by a newline, with no extra whitespace. A violation is any deviation from this format."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "The tests combine boundedness, achievability, monotonicity, and region‑specific invariants derived directly from the specification. Bounds follow from card values; achievability checks that the sum is feasible; monotonicity tests ensure that adding cards behaves as expected (a 1 or 0 cannot decrease the sum by more than 1, a –1 cannot increase it). The three region tests correspond to the greedy strategy's cases: when K≤A, when A≤K≤A+B, and when K≥A+B. Together they cover all code branches without re‑solving. If one test were dropped, it would be test_lower_bound, because it is very weak and unlikely to catch a subtle error.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound",
      "intent": "Assert that the returned sum does not exceed the number of 1‑cards available nor the number of cards drawn: sum ≤ min(A, K). A violation is a sum larger than this bound, which is impossible because each card contributes at most 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound",
      "intent": "Assert that the returned sum is at least –K, since each card contributes at least –1. A violation is a sum smaller than –K."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_achievable",
      "intent": "Verify that the returned sum S is achievable: there exist integers a≤A, b≤B, c≤C with a+b+c=K and a–c=S. Check feasibility by solving the linear constraints without iteration. A violation is an S that no selection of cards can produce."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity_K",
      "intent": "If K < A+B+C, run the program again with K+1. Assert that the absolute difference between the two sums is at most 1. A violation is a jump larger than 1, which cannot happen because adding one card changes the sum by at most 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity_A",
      "intent": "Increase A by 1 (keeping B,C,K unchanged) and run the program on the modified input. Assert that the new sum is not smaller than the original, and that the increase is at most 1. A violation would be a decrease or an increase greater than 1, contradicting that an extra 1‑card can improve the sum by at most 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity_B",
      "intent": "Increase B by 1 (keeping A,C,K unchanged) and run again. Assert that the new sum is not smaller and increases by at most 1. A violation would be a decrease or too large an increase, because an extra 0‑card can replace a –1 at most once."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity_C",
      "intent": "Increase C by 1 (keeping A,B,K unchanged) and run again. Assert that the new sum is not larger. A violation would be an increase, because an extra –1 card never improves the maximum sum."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_K_le_A",
      "intent": "If K ≤ A, assert that the returned sum equals K. A violation is any other value, because taking K ones yields sum K and no higher sum is possible."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_A_le_K_le_AplusB",
      "intent": "If A ≤ K ≤ A+B, assert that the returned sum equals A. A violation is a sum different from A, since taking all A ones and filling the rest with zeros gives sum A and no larger sum can be obtained."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_K_ge_AplusB",
      "intent": "If K ≥ A+B, assert that sum + K equals 2A + B. A violation is a sum that does not satisfy this linear relation, which follows from taking all A ones, all B zeros, and the remaining cards as –1s."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "These tests combine invariants, certificates, and metamorphic relations to catch backdoors. Invariants (1,2,10) ensure basic consistency with constraints. Certificates (3,4,7) verify the claimed answer using different computations, catching errors in the check function or binary search. Metamorphic tests (5,6,8,9) check robustness to input transformations: order independence, monotonicity in m, and two extreme cases that must yield 1 day. The most expendable is test 7 (simulation) as it overlaps with test 3 but provides an alternative implementation; dropping it would still leave strong coverage.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_output_format",
      "intent": "Verify that the output is either '-1' or an integer between 1 and n inclusive. A violation is any other string or an integer outside [1, n]."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_impossibility",
      "intent": "If the sum of all a_i is less than m, the output must be '-1'; otherwise, the output must not be '-1'. A violation is output '-1' when sum >= m, or a number when sum < m."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_sufficiency",
      "intent": "If the output is an integer d, sort a descending and compute total = sum(max(0, a_i - i//d)) over i. Assert total >= m. A violation is d being too small to achieve m."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_minimality",
      "intent": "If the output is an integer d > 1, sort a descending and compute total_prev = sum(max(0, a_i - i//(d-1))). Assert total_prev < m. A violation is d not being minimal (d-1 also sufficient)."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_order_independence",
      "intent": "Create a modified input by reversing the order of a_i (keeping n,m unchanged). Run the program on it and assert the output equals the original. A violation is different outputs for the same multiset."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonicity_m",
      "intent": "If m < sum(a), create a modified input with m' = m+1 (same n,a). Run and let d' be the output. If original output d is not -1, assert d' >= d. If original is -1, assert d' is not -1. A violation is decreasing days when m increases."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_greedy_simulation",
      "intent": "If output is d, sort a descending. Assign cups to d days round-robin, compute each day's contribution as sum of max(0, cup_value - position_in_day) for cups sorted descending within the day, sum over days. Assert total >= m. A violation is d insufficient."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_add_max_cup",
      "intent": "Create a modified input by appending a cup with caffeine m to a, increment n by 1, keep m unchanged. Run and assert output is '1'. A violation is not 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_m_eq_1",
      "intent": "Create a modified input with m set to 1 (same n,a). Run and assert output is '1'. A violation is not 1."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_max_ge_m",
      "intent": "If max(a) >= m, assert output is '1'. A violation is output not 1 when a cup alone can cover m."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "These tests target common backdoors, especially the cap at 100 evident in the submission. Boundedness, feasibility, and frequency lower bound enforce basic constraints. Invariance under reversal and shift catch unintended dependencies on order or absolute values. Monotonicity tests detect errors in incremental updates. The all-equal and large-balanced-team tests directly expose size caps, while isolated addition verifies handling of outliers. If one test were dropped, it would be test_monotonic_remove, as it is similar to test_monotonic_add and less likely to reveal unique errors.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_bounds",
      "intent": "Assert that the output is an integer between 1 and n inclusive. A violation is an output outside this range, which would contradict the trivial bounds implied by the problem statement."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_feasibility",
      "intent": "Given the output k, verify that there exists a set of k students with pairwise skill differences at most 5. This can be checked by sorting the skills and finding a contiguous subarray of length k with max-min ≤ 5. A violation indicates that the claimed maximum size is not achievable."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_max_frequency",
      "intent": "Compute the maximum frequency of any skill value. The output must be at least this frequency because all students with the same skill can form a balanced team. A violation would be an output smaller than this obvious lower bound."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_invariant_reversal",
      "intent": "Reverse the order of the input list and run the program again. The outputs must be equal because the problem depends only on the multiset of skills. A difference would indicate an order dependency that should not exist."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_invariant_shift",
      "intent": "Shift all skills by subtracting (min-1) so the new minimum is 1 (keeping them within [1,10^9]). Run the program on the shifted input. The output must remain unchanged because the condition depends only on differences. A change would indicate sensitivity to absolute values."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonic_add",
      "intent": "Append a student with skill equal to the current minimum (ensuring n+1 ≤ 2·10^5). Run the program on the enlarged input. The new output must be at least the original output, because adding a student cannot reduce the maximum team size. A decrease would violate monotonicity."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonic_remove",
      "intent": "Remove the first student from the input (if n>1). Run the program on the reduced input. The new output must be at most the original output, because removing a student cannot increase the maximum team size. An increase would violate monotonicity."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_all_equal",
      "intent": "If all skill values are identical, the output must equal n, since the whole set is balanced. A violation would be any other output, indicating an error in handling uniform inputs."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_isolated_addition",
      "intent": "Add a student whose skill differs by more than 5 from every existing skill (if such a value exists within [1,10^9]). Run the program on the new input. The output must equal the original output, because the new student cannot join any team and does not create a larger team. A difference would indicate incorrect handling of isolated values."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_large_balanced_team",
      "intent": "Construct an input with 200 students whose skills are all within the interval [1,5] (for example, repeating the sequence 1,2,3,4,5). The output must be 200, because the entire set is balanced. A smaller output would indicate a cap on team size."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "rationale": "These tests verify the submission against the problem specification. The simulation test ensures the logic matches the rules. Format and invariants catch malformed outputs. Symmetry between Alex and Bob is required due to identical initial roles. Prefix properties follow from the fact that a possible sequence requires all prefixes possible. Edge cases (n=1, first winner 3, constant sequences) target common pitfalls. Spectator consistency and necessary condition tests provide additional checks on YES and NO outputs. If a test must be dropped, test_prefix_impossible is somewhat redundant with test_prefix_possible and simulation.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_correctness",
      "intent": "Simulate the tournament process from the specification: start with Carl (3) spectating; for each game, if the winner is the current spectator, the sequence is impossible; otherwise the spectator becomes the third person (6 - winner - current spectator). After processing all games, assert that the program's output matches the result of this simulation. A violation is any discrepancy."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_output_format",
      "intent": "Assert that the program's output is exactly the string 'YES\\n' or 'NO\\n'. Any other output, including extra whitespace or missing newline, is a violation."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_symmetry_1_2",
      "intent": "Create a modified input by swapping every 1 with 2 and every 2 with 1 in the winner sequence (leaving 3 unchanged). Run the program on this modified input and assert that its output is identical to the original output. A violation is differing outputs, which would break the symmetry between Alex and Bob."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_prefix_possible",
      "intent": "For each prefix of the input (from the first game up to each length k), run the program on that prefix. If the original output is 'YES\\n', assert that every prefix also outputs 'YES\\n'. A violation is any prefix that outputs 'NO\\n' while the full sequence outputs 'YES\\n'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_prefix_impossible",
      "intent": "For each prefix of the input, run the program on that prefix. If any prefix outputs 'NO\\n', assert that the original output is also 'NO\\n'. A violation is the full sequence outputting 'YES\\n' while a prefix outputs 'NO\\n'."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_first_winner_not_3",
      "intent": "Check the first game's winner. If it is 3, assert that the program outputs 'NO\\n', because Carl cannot win the first game as he is spectating. A violation is outputting 'YES\\n' when the first winner is 3."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_single_game",
      "intent": "If the input consists of exactly one game (n==1), assert that the output is 'YES\\n' if the winner is 1 or 2, and 'NO\\n' if the winner is 3. A violation is any other output for a single-game input."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_constant_sequence",
      "intent": "If all winners in the sequence are the same number w, assert that the output is 'YES\\n' if w is 1 or 2, and 'NO\\n' if w is 3. A violation is outputting YES for all-3 or NO for all-1 or all-2."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_spectator_consistency",
      "intent": "If the program outputs 'YES\\n', compute the spectator sequence starting from 3 and updating by the rule spec = 6 - winner - spec for each game. Assert that at no point does the winner equal the computed spectator. A violation is a YES output where some winner equals the spectator at that game."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_necessary_condition",
      "intent": "Simulate the tournament correctly; if at any point the winner equals the current spectator, assert that the program's output is 'NO\\n'. A violation is outputting 'YES\\n' when such an impossibility occurs."
    }
  ]
}

