{
  "rationale": "These tests verify properties that every correct answer must satisfy, derived directly from the problem statement. Coverage and minimality check the definition of the minimum time. Symmetry and monotonicity are metamorphic relations based on reflection and adding taps. Lower bounds from ends and gaps are necessary conditions. Upper bound and integer are basic sanity checks. Special-case tests for all taps and single tap target common edge cases. If forced to drop one, test_integer would be the first, as it is the weakest and likely already enforced by the judge system.",
  "plan": [
    {
      "name": "test_coverage",
      "intent": "Given the output T, verify that for every garden bed i from 1 to n, there exists at least one tap position j such that |j - i| <= T - 1. A violation indicates a bed that would not be watered after T seconds, so the output is insufficient."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_minimality",
      "intent": "Verify that T is minimal. If T > 1, there must exist at least one bed i such that for every tap j, |j - i| >= T - 1 (i.e., the minimum distance from i to any tap is at least T - 1), showing T - 1 seconds are insufficient. If T = 1, verify that every bed has a tap (i.e., for each i there is a tap at i). A violation means T is not the minimum possible time."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_symmetry",
      "intent": "Create a new test case by reversing the garden: replace each tap position x with n + 1 - x, keeping n and k the same. Run the program on this input and assert that the output equals the original. A violation indicates asymmetry in handling left and right edges."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_monotonic_add_tap",
      "intent": "If k < n, add an extra tap at a position not already occupied (e.g., the leftmost empty bed), ensuring the tap list remains sorted. Run the program on this modified input and assert the new output is less than or equal to the original. A violation would mean adding a tap increased the required time, contradicting monotonicity of coverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_ends",
      "intent": "Assert that the output T is at least max(tap[0], n - tap[-1] + 1). These are the times needed to water the farthest beds at the two ends from the nearest tap. A violation means T is too small to cover an end."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_lower_bound_gaps",
      "intent": "For every pair of consecutive taps (x_i, x_{i+1}), compute d = (x_{i+1} - x_i) // 2 + 1. Assert that T is at least the maximum of these values over all gaps. This is a lower bound derived from the worst-case bed between two taps. A violation indicates T is too small to cover some gap."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_upper_bound_n",
      "intent": "Assert that the output T is at most n. Since a single tap at an end can water the entire garden in n seconds, T cannot exceed n. A violation indicates an implausibly large time."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_integer",
      "intent": "Verify that the output is a valid integer. This is a basic sanity check; a non-integer output would be invalid for a number of seconds."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_all_taps",
      "intent": "If k equals n (every bed has a tap), assert that the output is exactly 1. Because all beds are watered at time 1. A violation would be any other value."
    },
    {
      "name": "test_single_tap",
      "intent": "If k equals 1, compute expected = max(tap[0], n - tap[0] + 1). Assert that the output equals expected. This is the exact answer for the single-tap case. A violation indicates an error in handling this special case."
    }
  ]
}

{
